Here's the actual tradeoff, without the one-sided pitch.

## The tradeoff, side by side

{% table %}
* Factor
* Agency
* In-House SDR
---
* Time to first meeting booked
* 3 to 4 weeks on average: campaigns go live within 18 days because systems and infrastructure are already built
* Typically 3 to 6 months once you count hiring, onboarding, and ramp-up before the first meeting lands
---
* Upfront cost
* A monthly retainer. No recruiting cost, no salary, no benefits before you see a result
* Recruiter fees plus a base salary (commonly $60K to $100K+ fully loaded, depending on market) before a single meeting is booked
---
* Ramp-up time
* Live within days, the ICP, sequences, and sending infrastructure already exist
* 60 to 90 days is typical for a new SDR to reach full productivity, longer if they're also learning your product
---
* Tooling & infrastructure
* Included and managed: domain warm-up, deliverability monitoring, enrichment tools, reporting dashboards
* You buy, configure, and maintain all of it yourself, or it becomes one more thing on someone's plate
---
* Risk if it doesn't work
* Contract flexibility, month-to-month after a 3-month minimum engagement
* Severance, sunk hiring cost, and months of lost ramp-up time if the hire doesn't work out
---
* Scalability
* Scale volume up or down with a conversation, not a hiring cycle
* Scaling means repeating the entire hire-train-ramp cycle for every additional rep
{% /table %}

Time-to-first-meeting and minimum engagement figures are Myntmore's actual averages. In-house salary and ramp-up ranges are typical industry figures, not Myntmore-specific data, since we don't run your hiring process.

## When in-house actually makes more sense

Your sales motion is deeply technical or relationship-led, and prospects need to feel like they're talking to someone who lives inside your product every day, not an external rep working several accounts at once.

You're playing a long game. A great SDR who grows into an AE, learns your product cold, and builds real relationships with your accounts can compound in value over 2+ years in a way a retainer doesn't.

You already have the infrastructure, tooling, and management bandwidth to ramp someone properly, and outbound is a core, strategic capability you want fully owned and built as internal IP.

You have the runway to absorb 3 to 6 months of near-zero output while a hire ramps, without that gap threatening your pipeline.

## When an agency actually makes more sense

You need pipeline now, not in a quarter. Hiring, onboarding, and ramp-up realistically take months before a new SDR is fully productive.

You're testing a new market, ICP, or offer and don't yet know if it's worth a full-time hire. It's far cheaper to learn that with a flexible engagement than with a severance package.

You don't have (and don't want to build) in-house expertise in deliverability infrastructure, list enrichment, or outbound tooling, that's a specialised skill set on its own.

You want a predictable monthly cost instead of a hiring outcome you can't fully control, some hires simply don't work out, no matter how good the interview process is.

